From: Qianzf <qianzf@example.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 10:45:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] AP: restart the BSS when its configuration changed

hostapd can only apply a subset of the BSS configuration to a running BSS.
The SSID, BSSID, HT/VHT/HE/EHT capabilities, bridge, PMF and several other
items are programmed into the driver only when the AP is started, and
hostapd_set_ssid() is a no-op for AP mode.  The in-place reload path
therefore leaves the BSS in a torn state: the beacon and the driver keep
the old parameters while hapd->conf carries the new ones.

Observed on ath12k (ipq5332, OpenWrt): after changing the SSID and applying
it, the AP keeps beaconing the old name, clients connect to it and are
rejected with

  wlan0-0: STA ... Station tried to associate with unknown SSID 'old-name'

or associate successfully and are then deauthenticated due to inactivity.
Wi-Fi looks connected (association and 4-way handshake succeed) but carries
no data until the interface is restarted completely.

The reload path already detects this situation through the per-BSS
config_id (the md5 of the whole generated BSS block) and flushes all
stations, but it still attempts the in-place reload afterwards.  Instead,
take the full disable+enable path so the new configuration reaches the
driver through start_ap again.  Interface/radio level changes keep the
previous behaviour for forced reloads, as the caller restarts the
interface itself in that case.

The shared reload event is sent before the reload because the interface is
torn down and re-created, which frees hapd.

Signed-off-by: Qianzf <qianzf@example.com>
---
 src/ap/hostapd.c | 47 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 src/ap/ubus.c    | 17 ++++++++++++-----
 2 files changed, 58 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

--- a/src/ap/hostapd.c
+++ b/src/ap/hostapd.c
@@ -276,6 +276,47 @@
 }
 
 
+/*
+ * hostapd can only apply a subset of the BSS configuration to a running BSS:
+ * the SSID, BSSID, HT/VHT/HE/EHT capabilities, bridge, PMF and a number of
+ * other items are programmed into the driver only when the AP is started.
+ * hostapd_set_ssid() in particular is a no-op for AP mode, so an in-place
+ * reload keeps the old SSID in the beacon and in the driver while
+ * hapd->conf carries the new one - clients then associate with the old name
+ * and are rejected as "unknown SSID" (or deauthenticated for inactivity),
+ * i.e. Wi-Fi looks connected but carries no data.
+ *
+ * OpenWrt's hostapd.sh emits config_id as the md5 over the whole generated
+ * BSS block, so any BSS parameter change is visible here.  When no config_id
+ * is present (plain wpa_supplicant build), fall back to comparing the SSID,
+ * which is the change users hit most often.
+ */
+static int hostapd_bss_conf_changed(struct hostapd_config *newconf,
+				    struct hostapd_config *oldconf)
+{
+	size_t i;
+
+	for (i = 0; i < newconf->num_bss && i < oldconf->num_bss; i++) {
+		if (newconf->bss[i]->config_id && oldconf->bss[i]->config_id) {
+			if (os_strcmp(newconf->bss[i]->config_id,
+				      oldconf->bss[i]->config_id) != 0)
+				return 1;
+			continue;
+		}
+
+		if (newconf->bss[i]->ssid.ssid_len !=
+		    oldconf->bss[i]->ssid.ssid_len)
+			return 1;
+
+		if (os_memcmp(newconf->bss[i]->ssid.ssid,
+			      oldconf->bss[i]->ssid.ssid,
+			      newconf->bss[i]->ssid.ssid_len) != 0)
+			return 1;
+	}
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
 static int hostapd_iface_conf_changed(struct hostapd_config *newconf,
 				      struct hostapd_config *oldconf)
 {
@@ -303,6 +344,7 @@
 	struct hapd_interfaces *interfaces = iface->interfaces;
 	struct hostapd_data *hapd = iface->bss[0];
 	struct hostapd_config *newconf, *oldconf;
+	int bss_changed;
 	size_t j;
 	int i;
 
@@ -322,11 +364,19 @@
 		return -1;
 
 	oldconf = hapd->iconf;
-	if (hostapd_iface_conf_changed(newconf, oldconf)) {
+	bss_changed = hostapd_bss_conf_changed(newconf, oldconf);
+	if (hostapd_iface_conf_changed(newconf, oldconf) || bss_changed) {
 		char *fname;
 		int res;
 
-		if (reconf)
+		/*
+		 * A BSS level change must be applied for real, otherwise the
+		 * BSS keeps running with the old parameters (see
+		 * hostapd_bss_conf_changed()).  Interface/radio level changes
+		 * keep the previous behaviour for forced reloads: the caller
+		 * restarts the interface itself.
+		 */
+		if (reconf && !bss_changed)
 			return -1;
 
 		hostapd_clear_old(iface);
--- a/src/ap/ubus.c
+++ b/src/ap/ubus.c
@@ -203,9 +203,18 @@
 		   struct blob_attr *msg)
 {
 	struct hostapd_data *hapd = container_of(obj, struct hostapd_data, ubus.obj);
-	int ret = hostapd_reload_config(hapd->iface, 1);
+	struct hostapd_iface *iface = hapd->iface;
+	const char *ifname = hapd->conf->iface;
+	int ret;
 
-	hostapd_send_shared_event(&hapd->iface->interfaces->ubus, hapd->conf->iface, "reload");
+	/*
+	 * Send the event before the reload: a BSS configuration change makes
+	 * hostapd_reload_config() tear down and re-create the interface, which
+	 * frees hapd (and its conf).
+	 */
+	hostapd_send_shared_event(&iface->interfaces->ubus, ifname, "reload");
+
+	ret = hostapd_reload_config(iface, 1);
 	return ret;
 }
 
